This PR turns off `strict` in the app's tsconfig. Nothing in the feature needs that, and every other app in the monorepo (`admin-x-settings`, `posts`, `stats`, `shade`, …) keeps `strict: true`.

::diff{file="apps/activitypub/tsconfig.json" lines="18-18"}

::callout{type="warning"}
With strict off, `strictNullChecks` and `noImplicitAny` stop applying to the entire app. This PR also changes `blueskyHandle` to `string | null`, which is exactly the kind of change null checking exists to catch. Turning strict off probably hides type errors that the new code introduces. It doesn't fix them.

::suggestion{file="apps/activitypub/tsconfig.json" lines="18-18"}
    "strict": true,

The old code wrapped the mutation in `try/finally { setLoading(false) }`. That's gone now:

::diff{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="48-55"}

If `enableBlueskyMutation.mutateAsync()` rejects (500, network error, 429), the cache is never updated, so `account.blueskyEnabled` stays `false`. The `useEffect` only depends on `blueskyEnabled`/`blueskyHandleConfirmed`, so it never re-runs to reset `loading`. The button stays disabled with a spinner and the "You can leave this page…" hint until the user navigates away. The rejection is also unhandled: `handleEnable` is an `onClick` handler, and nothing awaits it.

::suggestion{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="52-53"}
            setLoading(true);
            try {
                await enableBlueskyMutation.mutateAsync();
            } catch {
                setLoading(false);
                toast.error('Something went wrong, please try again.');
            }

`confirmHandle` fires `mutateAsync().then(...)` with no `.catch`. Any confirm-handle failure (500, network blip) becomes an unhandled rejection on every 5s tick:

::diff{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="68-75"}

The auto-disable branch has the same problem. If `disableBlueskyMutation.mutateAsync()` throws, `setLoading(false)` never runs, the interval is already cleared, and the UI stays in the loading state after showing the error toast:

::code{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="103-115"}

The ticks also don't wait for the previous request to finish. On a slow backend, confirm requests can overlap.

::suggestion{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="69-73"}
        confirmBlueskyHandleMutation.mutateAsync().then((handle) => {
            if (handle) {
                setHandleConfirmed(true);
            }
        }).catch(() => {
            // Treat as not-yet-confirmed; the next interval tick will retry
        });

`showAsEnabled` already requires `account.blueskyHandleConfirmed`. `handleConfirmed` mirrors that same value from the effect and from the `confirmHandle` callback, but it starts as `false`. When the page mounts for an already-confirmed account, the first paint shows the 'connected' copy without the profile card. The card only appears after the effect runs.

::diff{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="190-190"}

Inside the `showAsEnabled` branch, `handleConfirmed` is always true once the effect has run, so the extra state only adds a render of lag and gives the confirmation status a second source of truth. Consider dropping `handleConfirmed` and rendering the card whenever `showAsEnabled` is true.

This comment says an invalidation "should happen here" but doesn't do it:

::diff{file="apps/activitypub/src/hooks/use-activity-pub-queries.ts" lines="2833-2833"}

The brid.gy follow happens in the enable step, and `useEnableBlueskyMutationForUser` already invalidates `accountFollows('index', 'following')`. Either the invalidation really is needed here (add it), or the comment is wrong and should be removed. As it stands, a future reader can't tell which.
